<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Purge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Great Terror, Year of '37, Yezhovshchina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Purge, or Great Terror, also called the Yezhovshchina, was a political purge in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from 1936 to 1939. After the assassination of Sergei <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirov">Kirov</a> in 1934, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> launched the show trials known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> trials, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> removed Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-bukharin">Nikolai Bukharin</a>, Lev Kamenev and Grigory Zinoviev, officials, party bosses and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> high command. At its peak, from September 1936 to August 1938 under NKVD chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a>, hundreds of thousands were accused of political crimes and shot or sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> labour camps, and minorities such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-germans">Volga Germans</a> were deported. Scholars estimate the death toll at 700,000 to 1.2 million.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-purge.jpg" alt="Great Purge" width="330" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Soviet government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Purge_Resolution_of_Central_Committee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>A power vacuum opened in the Communist Party after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>'s death in 1924. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, the party's general secretary, triumphed over his opponents by 1928 and gained control of the party. His main adversary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a>, was forced into exile in 1929. In the early 1930s party officials began to lose faith in Stalin's leadership, largely because of the human cost of the first five-year plan and the collectivisation of agriculture, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a> famine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Between 1936 and 1938 three large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> trials of former senior party leaders were held. The defendants were accused of conspiring with fascist and capitalist powers to assassinate Stalin, dismember the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, and forced confessions helped to obtain convictions. Trotsky was tried in absentia and sentenced to death for treason; historians have found no evidence to support the charge. The third trial, in March 1938, tried 21 defendants including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-bukharin">Nikolai Bukharin</a>, Alexei Rykov and the recently disgraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> head <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genrikh-yagoda">Genrikh Yagoda</a>.</p><p>The purge reached beyond the party. On 2 July 1937 Stalin ordered regional party and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> chiefs to estimate the number of kulaks and criminals in their districts, who were to be arrested and shot or sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>. Mass operations against nationalities followed in 1937 and 1938; the Polish Operation was the largest, with 143,810 arrests and 111,091 executions. In the army and navy the purge removed three of five marshals, 13 of 15 army commanders and eight of nine admirals. Similar purges took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, where about 18,000 Buddhist lamas were killed, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In the summer of 1938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a> was relieved as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> and was eventually tried and executed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a> succeeded him. A decree of 17 November 1938 cancelled most orders of systematic repression and suspended the carrying out of death sentences. Official figures put verifiable executions in 1937 and 1938 at 681,692, and estimates of total deaths range up to 1.2 million. Historians with archive access have confirmed Stalin's close involvement: he signed 357 lists in those two years authorising about 40,000 executions.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> denounced the purges in his secret speech to the 20th party congress in February 1956, acknowledging that many victims were innocent and convicted on confessions obtained by torture. Historians have since disagreed over how to explain the terror: the totalitarian school stressed fear as an instrument of control, while revisionists emphasised rival factions exploiting Stalin's paranoia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Purge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through collectivisation, the <b>Great Purge</b>, the Second World War and the return of genocide survivors it became an industrial republic of 3.3 million, and the Karabakh movement of 1988 led to its independence in 1991.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet Georgia lost thousands in the <b>Great Purge</b> of 1936–1938 and an estimated 350,000 of the 700,000 Georgians who fought in the Second World War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The darker legacy includes the Red Terror, collectivisation, the Gulag, the <b>Great Terror</b> of 1937–1938 and the deportation of whole peoples such as the Chechens, Kalmyks, Crimean Tatars and Volga Germans during the Second World War, whose consequences still shape relations in the North Caucasus and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…people, Soviet prisoners of war and others; in the Soviet Union some 680,000 people were shot in the <b>Great Terror</b> of 1937–1938, and millions passed through the camps of the Gulag; the famine caused by the Great Leap Forward in China killed tens of millions between 1959 and…</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Soviet Union millions died in the famine of 1932–1933, which struck Ukraine and Kazakhstan hardest; about 680,000 people were shot in the <b>Great Terror</b> of 1937–1938, and millions passed through the Gulag labour camps.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Great Purge</b> of 1936–1938 Stalin set out to destroy any possible opposition in the party, and old Bolsheviks such as Kamenev, Zinoviev, Rykov and Bukharin were tried and executed; the secret police went on sending suspects to the gulag.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
